  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 207 PM CST Tue Nov 24 2026\n\nToday - Wednesday: Cool and Dry\n\nAn upper level low currently can be seen on water vapor imagery in \nthe northern Plains. This low will move down into the middle \nMississippi River Valley by Wednesday evening. At the surface an \narea of high pressure sits over the region resulting in light winds \nthrough Wednesday. This upper level low keeps the cooler air around \nfor a couple of days. Highs stay in the mid 30s with overnight low \ntemperatures in the mid teens to mid 20s. Light snow is possible \nwith this shortwave on Wednesday, but will stay west of I-35. There \nis a low chance, <10% that some flurries may fall as this wave \ncontinues its trek south. The one problem that these flurries may \nface in their effort to reach the ground is the strong dry layer \nmake its way through the region. This is caused by a strong dry \nlayer between 500 and 850mb. Other than the low potential for \nflurries expect a dry forecast.\n\nThanksgiving: Precipitation Potential\n\nThe forecast for this years Thanksgiving storm continues to be a \ncomplex one. Two different shortwaves merge together in the middle \nMississippi River Valley on Thursday and then shift off to the \nnortheast on Friday. While cooler air filters in around the combined \nupper level low on Wednesday, the connection to the cooler Canadian \nair gets cutoff thanks to some warm air advection in the eastern US \nand western US coming together north of the Great Lakes. As a \nresult, this upper level low becomes cutoff and gradually warms \nthrough the day on Thanksgiving. With this warming, surface \ntemperatures stay at or just above freezing for many locations. That \nbeing said, snow still is expected to occur in a narrow location \nthanks to a stalled warm front Wednesday night into Thanksgiving \nDay. This band of snow remains in a north/south axis until the \nsurface low begins its shift off to the northeast, which occurs \nlater that afternoon/evening. \n\nThe impacts from this system still look like they will stay in \nwestern Illinois and southeastern Iowa. The timing for when portions \nof our CWA could see some light snow would be Thanksgiving morning \nwhen temperatures stay at or below freezing and when the best \nmoisture surge occurs. Still, the probabilities for measurable \nsnowfall are low, based on the latest LREF (10 to 30%), for portions \nof northeast Iowa and southwest Wisconsin. Now with this being a \ncomplex system there remains some uncertainty regarding the exact \ntrack this upper level low will take. If it can take a slightly more \nnorthern track, then more locations in our CWA could see some light \nsnow Thanksgiving morning. One thing to remember though is that even \nif it takes a more northern track, the upper level low is warming up \ndue to it being cutoff, so temperatures will still be at or above \nfreezing through the day. All in all, most locations will likely not \nsee any snow, but areas south of I-90 could see some light snow (10 \nto 30%) Thanksgiving morning.\n\nThe Weekend: Mild with Low Precipitation Chances\n\nShortwave ridging moves over the region for Friday with zonal flow \nfor Saturday. A weak front passes through the Upper Midwest Friday \nnight into Saturday morning bringing about a low chance for \nrain/snow showers (15 to 25%) as temperatures stay around the \nfreezing mark. High temperatures for Friday increase into the mid \n40s and by Saturday highs get into the mid to upper 40s with some \nlow 50s possible. Ensemble and deterministic models support a \nshortwave coming down Sunday, however there are differences in the \nstrength of this wave and how much precipitation could fall.",
